<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="topic-1-3-responsible-ai">
  <time minutes="45" />
  <title>Responsible AI and Human Decision-Making</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection>
    <title>Learning Objectives</title>
    <remark>
      <title>1.3.A</title>
      <p>
        Explain how human decisions shape the development, deployment, and maintenance of AI tools.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>1.3.B</title>
      <p>
        Evaluate the appropriate level of automation or augmentation for a task.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Human Decisions in AI</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.1</title>
      <p>
        AI tools can affect everyday choices such as what people watch, buy, and learn.
        Understanding the human decisions behind AI helps users evaluate outputs critically and
        helps developers design and maintain tools that are safer, more accurate, and more fair.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.2</title>
      <p>
        Artificial intelligence (AI) refers to computer systems that use data and patterns to
        perform tasks that are often associated with human intelligence, producing outputs such
        as predictions, recommendations, decisions, or generated content.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.3</title>
      <p>
        In AI systems, a model is the part of the system used to produce outputs from inputs.
        Models may follow rules created by people or learn patterns from data.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.4</title>
      <p>
        Many modern AI models are created by training on examples.
        The quality and representativeness of the training data can affect how accurate or fair the
        model’s outputs are.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Generative AI is a type of AI that creates new content, such as text, images, music, or code,
        based on patterns it has learned from examples.
        Some generative AI tools are powered by large language models (LLMs), which generate text by
        predicting likely sequences of words based on patterns learned from large collections of text.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.6</title>
      <p>
        Humans decide what happens with an AI tool’s output, including when to trust it,
        what actions to take based on it, and when a human should review or make the final decision.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.7</title>
      <p>
        When AI tools are used to create or transform content, humans must consider what content
        may be used and how outputs may be shared, including following licenses and giving attribution
        when required.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.8</title>
      <p>
        AI tools can generate realistic images, audio, or video that may be mistaken for authentic
        media (deepfakes).
        Humans must make decisions about safeguards, such as disclosure, verification, and responsible
        sharing, to reduce misinformation and harm.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.9</title>
      <p>
        Model drift occurs when a model’s performance changes over time because the real-world data
        or conditions it encounters shift, potentially making the model less accurate.
        AI systems should be monitored by humans to detect model drift. When drift is detected,
        humans can update data, adjust the model, or retrain the system to reduce inaccurate or
        harmful outcomes.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.A.10</title>
      <p>
        Developers can increase transparency by clearly describing an AI tool’s purpose, how it
        was developed, trained, and evaluated, and its limitations and appropriate uses.
        This allows users to make informed choices.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Automation and Augmentation</title>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.1</title>
      <p>
        Decisions about automation and augmentation affect everyday experiences, such as recommendations,
        autocorrect suggestions, and navigation directions.
        Understanding whether a system is acting automatically or supporting human decision-making
        helps users decide how much to rely on its output and when they should verify it, especially
        when errors, bias, or other limitations could affect the result.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.2</title>
      <p>
        Automation allows computers to carry out tasks with limited human involvement, often making
        work faster, more consistent, and more efficient.
        Automated systems can handle routine tasks and, in some cases, more complex ones.
        They may still produce inaccurate results when inputs are ambiguous, conditions change, or
        unusual cases are not handled well.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.3</title>
      <p>
        Accuracy is the degree to which an output or code solution matches defined criteria for
        correctness, such as a program’s requirements or known correct outputs.
        Accuracy can be evaluated by testing with defined inputs and comparing actual results to
        expected results.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.4</title>
      <p>
        Augmentation uses tools or systems to support human work while humans remain responsible for
        decisions and outcomes.
        In some cases, augmentation involves AI, such as in human-in-the-loop systems where AI outputs
        are reviewed by humans to improve accuracy and accountability.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.5</title>
      <p>
        The appropriateness of automation or augmentation depends on the context and the consequences
        of error.
        Although automation can be faster, more consistent, or easier to scale, it may be less appropriate
        in situations that are high-stakes, ambiguous, or require human judgment, empathy, or discretion.
        In those situations, augmentation may be preferred so that humans remain responsible for the final decision.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.6</title>
      <p>
        Evaluating the appropriate level of automation or augmentation requires weighing benefits such
        as speed or efficiency against risks such as bias, inaccuracy, privacy, or overreliance,
        including how thresholds affect outcomes.
        It also means considering social, environmental, and economic trade-offs such as impacts on people,
        resource and energy use, costs, labor, and access.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>1.3.B.7</title>
      <p>
        Evaluating automation or augmentation should consider how the choice affects user experience,
        including trust, transparency, and whether users can reasonably decline or reduce automation,
        such as choosing manual controls or requesting human review when needed.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>
</section>
